<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bresse</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bresse is a historical region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, lying mostly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain">Ain department</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and the Jura, with Bourg-en-Bresse as its main town. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> in 1272 through marriage and remained Savoyard until the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> of 1601 ceded it to France. The region is known for its farming and especially for the Bresse chicken.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bresse.jpg" alt="Bresse" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poulet_de_Bresse_area_of_production_on_France_relief_location_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
